<description>
Delete each foreign-color object whose bounding box is contained by a larger object of another color.

Input structure
- The black pixels are the empty canvas, and every other color forms one or more 8-connected monochromatic objects.
- Some larger foreground objects act as loose U/C-like containers around smaller objects of different colors.
Rule
- Find all 8-connected monochromatic non-black objects.
- Recolor an object to black exactly when its bounding box is contained in the bounding box of a larger object of another color.
- Keep all other pixels unchanged.
Key insight
- The removable objects are identified by containment in another color's rectangular extent, not by their own color or shape.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="ba1aa698" lines="41-55">
<description>
Extrapolate the moving sprite from a horizontal sequence of shared-border frames into the next standalone frame.

Input structure
- The grid is a row of equal-width framed panels sharing their vertical border columns.
- Each panel has the same fill color and one identical interior sprite at the same local columns.
- The sprite's top row changes by a constant amount from panel to panel.

Rule
- Build one output panel with the same height, frame color, fill color, and sprite.
- Continue the sprite's diagonal motion one more panel to the right.
- Because the output is a standalone panel, advance by the full panel width, not the shared-border spacing.

Key insight
- The panels are consecutive frames of motion; the output is the next frame after the sequence.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="4df5b0ae" lines="27-41">
<description>
Pack the colored sprites along the bottom edge from smallest to largest.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background color and several monochrome 4-connected
foreground sprites; a full-grid one-cell frame may surround the scene.

Rule
Remove any full-grid rectangular frame, sort the remaining sprites by pixel
area from smallest to largest, and paste their bounding-box sprites left to
right with each sprite bottom-aligned.

Key insight
The original locations do not determine the final slots; each sprite's area
gives the packing order, while its shape and color are preserved.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="16de56c4" lines="9-34">
<description>
Fill periodic progressions that shoot from a unique grid edge.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.  Sparse non-bg dots of several colors.
- All repeating colors originate from the same grid edge (top, bottom,
  left, or right — the one with the most non-bg dots).
- Each repeating color has exactly 2 dots per shooting-direction line;
  their gap defines the step.
- Every non-bg dot lies on a shooting-direction line that contains a
  repeating color.

Rule:
- Determine the unique shooting edge; left/right → rows, top/bottom → columns.
- For each shooting-direction line, the repeating color has exactly 2 dots;
  step = gap between them, offset = first position mod step.
- A different-colored dot aligned to the same beat (pos % step == offset) is
  a companion: it absorbs the ray and becomes the fill color, limiting the
  fill range to the span between repeating and companion dots.
- An unaligned dot is a bystander and left untouched.
- Without a companion the repeating color fills the entire line.

Key insight: the shooting edge is identified by counting boundary dots;
the gap between the two marker dots is the period; a companion sharing the
modular class acts as a wall that stops the ray and dictates the paint color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="758abdf0" lines="54-69">
<description>
Advance short marker bars away from the full edge bar, turning completed two-pixel markers into edge-color marks at the opposite side.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- One non-background color forms a complete one-cell-thick bar on exactly one outer edge.
- The other foreground color appears only in the first two cells inward from that bar, lane by lane.

Rule
- In each lane perpendicular to the edge bar, a one-pixel marker adjacent to the bar grows one more cell inward.
- A two-pixel marker in those first two inward cells is cleared back to background.
- The cleared two-pixel marker is replaced by a two-pixel mark of the edge-bar color at the opposite side of the same lane.

Key insight
- The full edge bar defines both the orientation and the direction of the local two-step marker cycle.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="c3fa4749" lines="79-96">
<description>
Recolor each side-marked multicolor object embedded in a monochrome panel to its marker color.

Input structure:
- Same-color 4-connected components form monochrome panel regions.
- The bounding box of a panel may contain 4-connected non-panel-color objects.
- A target object touches exactly one side of its panel bbox, and all of its
  pixels on that side have one marker color.

Rule:
- For every target object, fill any panel-colored holes enclosed by the object.
- Recolor the object and filled holes to the side marker color.
- Leave all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
- A multicolor object's single contact side identifies both which object should
  be normalized and which color should replace it.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="a395ee82" lines="9-24">
<description>
Stamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.

Input structure:
- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).
- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).
- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.

Rule:
- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.
- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.
- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.
- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.

Key insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the "self" copy, which is uniquely recolored.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="5751f35e" lines="29-43">
<description>
Complete sparse nested rectangular color layers into solid rectangles.

Input structure
The background is the dominant color, and every foreground color marks one
axis-aligned rectangular layer; the layer bboxes have unique areas and are
nested from outermost to innermost.
Rule
Take each color's bbox as its completed rectangle, except that a child bbox
which already matches at least three sides of its parent's one-pixel inset
expands to that full inset; repaint the rectangles largest to smallest.
Key insight
The scattered pixels are samples of nested rectangular regions, so the
output is a clean redraw of those regions with inner colors overwriting
outer colors.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="465b7d93" lines="97-117">
<description>
Move the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular
  one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.
- The frame interior is initially background.

Rule:
- Clear the original sprite from its outside location.
- Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to
  interior extremes.
- Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of
  adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside
  those drawn segments.

Key insight:
The outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,
not a bitmap to scale by block replication.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="c9680e90" lines="52-67">
<description>
Move each mirrored bottom source pixel to the far end of its trail and redraw the top half as its reflection.

Input structure
- A solid non-background row separates equal-height top and bottom halves.
- The top half has one marker color on the background.
- In the bottom half, exactly one color has positions that mirror the top markers across the separator, and the other color forms trails touching those source pixels.

Rule
- Identify the bottom source color by reflecting each bottom color into the top half and matching the top marker set.
- For every bottom source pixel, follow its unique 4-connected trail of the other bottom color to the trail endpoint.
- Clear the old top markers, bottom sources, and trails; place the source color at each endpoint and place the top marker color at the reflected endpoint.

Key insight
- The top half is only a mirror display of the bottom moving sources; the actual motion is encoded by the trail attached to each bottom source pixel.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

